      {
        "caution_id": "evidence-gap:19c331487bc2e13092d28840",
        "description": "Universal hyperparameters: Reported latent dimensions, optimization epochs and architecture are study settings, not universal prerequisites or current defaults.",
        "evidence": [
          {
            "content_hash": "4bf3032d97ea661dfb4cb2fbec83ae91497257f7c4aee7e3b8241bad74fedff6",
            "end_offset": 29832,
            "evidence_span_id": "evidence-span:de1a441f1585580497e52634",
            "exact_text": "Throughout the paper, we use Adam (a first order stochastic optimizer) with ε = 0.01. As indicated in [27], we use deterministic warmup and batch normalization during learning to learn an expressive model. A complete list of hyperparameters is provided in Supplementary Table2. The hyperparameters were chosen using a small grid search that maximized held-out log likelihood—a common practice for training deep generative models. One of the strengths of scVI is that we have only three dataset-specific hyperparameters to set (learning rate, number of layers, and layer width). We optimize the objective function until convergence –usually between 120 and 250 epochs, where each epoch is a complete pass through the dataset (let us note that bigger datasets require fewer epochs). For the larger subset of the BRAIN-LARGE dataset, we also ran with the early stopping criterion: the algorithm stops after 12 consecutive epochs with no improvement on the validation loss.",
